<section class="pk-overview not-prose" aria-labelledby="product-knowledge-overview-title">
  <div class="pk-overview__intro">
    <p class="pk-overview__eyebrow">Overview · ~2 min</p>
    <h2 id="product-knowledge-overview-title">Give the “why” a place to live</h2>
    <p>
      Code implements behavior, application state holds current facts, and schemas define valid structure. Why a flow exists, which information matters, and what to explain when an action is unavailable often live in scattered documents and decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      This research asks whether that <strong>product knowledge</strong> can become explicit enough to help assemble parts of an interface from capabilities engineers already built—without becoming authority for facts, permission, policy enforcement, or execution.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="overview-boundary" aria-labelledby="product-knowledge-boundary-title">
    <div class="overview-boundary__heading">
      <h3 id="product-knowledge-boundary-title">An explanation is not a permission</h3>
      <p>Account review · identity verification has not succeeded</p>
    </div>

    <div class="overview-boundary__grid">
      <section class="overview-boundary__knowledge">
        <p class="overview-boundary__label accent">Product knowledge · may guide</p>
        <h4>Explain the next step</h4>
        <p>Show why verification matters and when a removal action is relevant.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="overview-boundary__authority">
        <p class="overview-boundary__label">State · policy · code · retain authority</p>
        <h4>Keep removal unavailable</h4>
        <p>State records verification; policy requires it; code enforces the action.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      Illustrative boundary, not an implemented interface. Guidance cannot change a fact, grant access, or perform an action.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="pk-overview__section">
    <h3 id="product-knowledge-task">A task, not a new power</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine an account-review surface. A product author can describe its purpose, relevant details, prerequisites, and helpful explanations. The system might select existing customer, verification, and restriction capabilities around that task.
    </p>
    <p>
      But a description cannot retrieve transactions, verify a person, authorize removal, or invent a verification capability that has not been built. Composition can arrange trusted capabilities; it cannot make missing behavior real.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="pk-overview__section pk-overview__closing">
    <h3 id="product-knowledge-open-question">The question still open</h3>
    <p>
      This is an architectural proposition, not a working generator or a case for generating every screen. Product knowledge would need clear ownership, conditions for when it applies, synchronization with code and policy, and tests that prove a proposed composition is allowed.
    </p>
    <p>
      If those boundaries hold, how much of a repeated workflow could be composed without giving up deliberate design or deterministic control?
    </p>

    <button class="pk-overview__continue" type="button" data-reading-depth-open="full">
      Full Research · ~8 min <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</section>

